      "page": 7,
      "text": "BASS Science Background\n• The BASS investigation tests the hypothesis that materials in microgravity burn as well\nif not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being\nidentical (ie. pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature).\n• To test this hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended\nmicrogravity time – longer than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. If this\nhypothesis is true, it implies serious safety concerns over the utility and margin of\nsafety provided NASA-STD-6001 Test #1 (upward flammability test used to evaluate\nmaterials intended for use in the habitable environments of US spacecraft). Practical,\nrealistic (thicker) fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including wake flames\nwhich have characteristics of a fire burning behind another object (for example, a fire\nburning in tight area which may not be easily accessible).\n• There are important differences in the suppression of fires in space compared to on\nEarth. On Earth it is understood that best results are generally obtained when the\nextinguisher “attacks” the base of the flame, which is both the stabilization point and\nthe point of fresh air entra",
