      "page": 3,
      "text": "February 2010 3\n\nBackground\n\nFlame spread behavior, flammability, and suppression data are of interest to NASA for\napplication to fire safety efforts. The reduced buoyant flows, coupled with low-speed air\ncirculation currents, create an environment quite different than normal Earth gravity. In\naddition, there are several oxidizer atmospheres proposed for crew-manned vehicles\nand habitats [NASA CxP-70024], which further complicates the problem.\nUnderstanding flammability and suppression in a variety of environments is especially\nimportant given NASA’s current exploration initiatives.\n\nThere is some evidence for the hypothesis that materials in microgravity, with adequate\nventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other\nconditions being identical (pressure, oxygen concentration, temperature, etc.) To test\nthis hypothesis with any but the thinnest materials requires extended microgravity time –\nlonger than available in drop towers or parabolic aircraft. If this hypothesis is true, it\nimplies serious safety concerns over the utility and margin of safety provided NASA-\nSTD-6001 Test #1.",
